---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../../components/Showcase.astro";
// The preview clones the <head> sheets of the page, but whatever the demo uses has to be
// imported here: `.sk-truncate` is from the demo, not the package.
import "../../../examples/tooltip.css";
import { TooltipDemo } from "../../../components/react-demos/tooltip";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/vanilla @skryensya/react`;

/*
 * Three triggers and nothing more: the three cases where a tooltip earns its place, without the dashboard that
 *before it surrounded them. Between one and the other only the shape of the trigger and the placement changes; the rest of the
 * markup is identical, which is just what you need to be able to read.
 *
 * Without layout container: `.sk-tooltip` is `inline-flex`, so the three flow in line where
 * whether they stick. A wrapper here would be markup that is not a tooltip.
 */
const html = `<div class="sk-inline" data-gap="md" data-align="center">
  <!-- 1. An icon-only control. The name lives in aria-label; the tooltip expands it. -->
  <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor data-sk-placement="block-end">
    <button
      class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor"
      type="button"
      data-variant="ghost"
      data-icon-only
      aria-label="Exportar"
      data-sk-anchor-trigger
    ><span data-sk-icon="download"></span></button>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
      <!-- The arrow, optional: is authored or does not exist. It comes out from the trigger, not from the center of the box. -->
      <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>Download the visible period in CSV</span>
    </span>
  </span>

  <!-- 2. Explain a piece of information that is already on the screen: the number is always seen, the tooltip says how it is calculated. -->
  <span class="sk-inline" data-gap="xs">
    Ingresos $48.2k
    <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor>
      <button
        class="sk-button sk-interactive sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor"
        type="button"
        data-variant="ghost"
        data-icon-only
        data-size="sm"
        aria-label="How Income is Calculated"
        data-sk-anchor-trigger
      ><span data-sk-icon="info"></span></button>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
        <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>Amount invoiced for the period, without taxes or refunds</span>
      </span>
    </span>
  </span>

  <!-- 3. Return what width had to be cut. The trigger is the truncated text itself. -->
  <span class="sk-tooltip" data-sk-anchor data-sk-placement="inline-end">
    <span
      class="sk-truncate sk-tooltip__trigger sk-anchor"
      tabindex="0"
      data-sk-anchor-trigger
    >Billing pipeline migration</span>
    <span class="sk-tooltip__positioner sk-anchored" data-sk-anchor-positioner>
      <span class="sk-anchored-arrow" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span class="sk-tooltip__content" data-sk-anchor-content>Billing pipeline migration</span>
    </span>
  </span>
</div>`;

const react = `import { Tooltip } from "@skryensya/react/tooltip";

function Example() {
  return (
    <div className="sk-inline" data-gap="md" data-align="center">
      {/* 1. An icon-only control. The name lives in aria-label, the tooltip expands it.
             \`arrow\` is the same as authoring <span className="sk-anchored-arrow" />
             inside the positioner in the HTML: optional in both bindings, and off
             by default. */}
      <Tooltip content="Download the visible period in CSV" placement="block-end" arrow>
        <button aria-label="Exportar" type="button">
          <DownloadIcon />
        </button>
      </Tooltip>

      {/* 2. Explain a piece of information that is already on the screen; this tells how it is calculated. */}
      <Tooltip content="Amount invoiced for the period, without taxes or refunds" arrow>
        <button aria-label="How Income is Calculated" type="button">
          <InfoIcon />
        </button>
      </Tooltip>

      {/* 3. Return what the width cut off. tabIndex makes truncated text
             reachable: without focus, the tooltip would be mouse-only. */}
      <Tooltip content={row.title} placement="inline-end" arrow>
        <span className="sk-truncate" tabIndex={0}>{row.title}</span>
      </Tooltip>
    </div>
  );
}`;
---

<Base
  title="Tooltip"
  description="Tooltip: an auxiliary description anchored to the trigger, with aria-describedby, Escape and CSS anchor positioning."
>
  <h1>Tooltip</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Tooltip is a <strong>auxiliary description</strong>, never the name of a control or the only place where a piece of data lives. The machine hangs <code>aria-describedby</code> from the trigger while it is open, not <code>aria-labelledby</code>: the control already has to have an accessible name on its own, and the tooltip extends it.
  </p>

  {/* `menu`: the tooltip paints outside the flow, and in a stage at the top of the content it would not have to
      where to go, so the actual placement would never be seen. */}
  <Showcase html={html} react={react} label="Tooltip" viewport="menu">
    <Fragment slot="react">
      <TooltipDemo client:load />
    </Fragment>
  </Showcase>

  <h2>The rule that the system cannot verify</h2>
  <p>
    There are two situations with no possible fix within the component. In <strong>touch</strong> there is no hover: the machine opens in <code>pointerenter</code> and in <code>focus</code>, so on a phone the tooltip practically does not appear. <strong>Without JavaScript</strong> the content is painted hidden and only the machine opens it, so it does not appear either.
  </p>
  <p>
    In both cases, no information is lost <em>because</em> the contract prohibits there being information there that is not elsewhere. A tooltip that is the only source of something is a bug of the person who uses it, and the validator cannot detect it: that is why it is written here and in the <code>@skryensya/core/tooltip</code> contract.
  </p>

  <h2>WCAG 1.4.13</h2>
  <p>
    The <em>Content on Hover or Focus</em> criterion requires three behaviors, all enabled by
    default. <strong>Dismissible</strong>: <kbd class="sk-kbd">Esc</kbd> closes the tooltip without
    moving the pointer or focus. <strong>Persistent</strong>: no timer closes it.
    <strong>Hoverable</strong>: the pointer can move onto the tooltip without making it disappear.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hoverability comes from the machine's <code>interactive</code> option, which is
    <strong>enabled</strong> by default. Disabling it gives the content
    <code>pointer-events: none</code>; the pointer cannot reach the tooltip before it closes, so the
    result fails the criterion. A descriptive tooltip may have nothing to click, but
    <em>hoverable</em> exists so people can <strong>read it</strong>, including people using screen
    magnification or living with tremors. You can opt out with
    <code>data-interactive="false"</code>, knowing that the result no longer meets the criterion.
  </p>

  <h2>Placement</h2>
  <p>
    <code>data-sk-placement</code> accepts four values on logical axes:
    <code>block-start</code> (the default), <code>block-end</code>, <code>inline-start</code>, and
    <code>inline-end</code>. They express a <em>preference</em>, not a guarantee. When the tooltip does
    not fit, it flips to the opposite side on the same axis: a request for <code>inline-end</code>
    still means “beside the trigger,” so moving above it would change the intent rather than adapt
    the placement.
  </p>

  <h2>Positioning</h2>
  <p>
    Where there is <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_anchor_positioning" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">CSS anchor positioning</a>, the browser places the tooltip: without layout loop and without measuring on each scroll. The enhancer stamps a single <code>anchor-name</code> and stops passing inline styles to Zag, so there are no two positioning engines fighting. Browsers without the API are left with Zag JS positioning, which is the fallback, not an inferior path.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>arrow</strong> follows the same distribution: it comes out of the <em>trigger</em> and not from the center of the box, so it continues pointing at the control even if the box has been moved so as not to leave the screen, and it turns with it. It lives inside the positioner and is still anchored to the trigger, because it is <code>fixed</code>: a fix is contained in the viewport, not its parent. The machine places it in the fallback. It is counted in <a href="/en/anchoring">Anchor</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />

  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>
</Base>
